  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///public/assets/aistudio/Paste_November_20__2026_-_12_43PM_32eb1cdd"
    end_line: 1599
    content: "# MASTER PROJECT PROMPT \342\200\224 TERMLINK\n\n## Project Name\n**TermLink**\n\n## Tagline\n**Connect terminals. Share files. Collaborate directly.**\n\n## Project Vision\n\nBuild a production-oriented, cross-platform, peer-to-peer terminal collaboration platform that allows people to connect their computers directly through terminal commands.\n\nTermLink should allow users to create rooms, invite others using extremely short 2-digit codes or shareable links, communicate with group members, transfer files, and collaboratively share terminal sessions.\n\nThe product must work across Windows, Linux, macOS, physical computers, cloud VMs, VMware virtual machines, VirtualBox VMs, and WSL where supported.\n\nThis is a real software product, not a landing-page demo, mockup, prototype UI, or simulated networking application.\n\nBuild a functional implementation with real networking, real data transfer, security, error handling, automated tests, documentation, and a usable command-line interface.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PRIMARY OBJECTIVES\n\nBuild TermLink with the following objectives:\n\n1. Extremely easy to use.\n2. Cross-platform compatibility.\n3. Peer-to-peer communication whenever possible.\n4. Group rooms with multiple connected peers.\n5. Very short default room joining codes.\n6. Secure communication.\n7. No mandatory user account for basic functionality.\n8. Minimal centralized infrastructure.\n9. Direct file transfers between peers.\n10. Optional relay fallback when direct connections fail.\n11. Interactive and read-only terminal sharing.\n12. Easy installation and distribution through npm.\n13. Reliable connection handling and reconnection.\n14. A modular architecture that can be expanded in the future.\n\n---\n\n# 2. TARGET PLATFORMS\n\nSupport the following operating systems and environments.\n\n## Windows\n\n- Windows 10\n- Windows 11\n- Windows Server where compatible\n- PowerShell\n- Windows Terminal\n- Command Prompt\n\n## Linux\n\n- Ubuntu\n- Debian\n- Fedora\n- Arch Linux\n- Kali Linux\n- Linux Mint\n- Other mainstream distributions\n\n## macOS\n\n- Intel Macs\n- Apple Silicon Macs\n- Terminal\n- iTerm2\n\n## Virtualization environments\n\n- VMware Workstation\n- VMware Fusion\n- VMware ESXi guest VMs\n- VirtualBox\n- Hyper-V\n- Cloud virtual machines\n- WSL2\n\nTermLink must run inside the guest operating system.\n\nVMware is not a separate platform-specific implementation. TermLink should work in a VMware guest using its operating system\'s native installation.\n\nSupport different network configurations where technically possible:\n\n- Bridged\n- NAT\n- Host-only\n- Custom virtual networks\n- VPN-connected machines\n\nDocument network limitations clearly.\n\n---\n\n# 3. CORE USER EXPERIENCE\n\nThe application must prioritize simplicity.\n\nThe ideal workflow:\n\n1. User installs TermLink.\n2. User executes `termlink`.\n3. A room is automatically created.\n4. A two-digit room code is displayed.\n5. A short invitation link is displayed.\n6. Other users run `termlink 47`.\n7. The host approves incoming participants.\n8. Participants become connected.\n9. Users can chat, share files, or collaborate.\n\nThe user should not need to understand networking, IP addresses, ports, NAT, WebRTC, or server configuration for normal usage.\n\n---\n\n# 4. DEFAULT QUICK CONNECT \342\200\224 TWO DIGIT CODE\n\nThe default joining method MUST use a two-digit code.\n\nValid codes:\n\n`00` through `99`\n\nThe code must be displayed prominently and be easy to remember and communicate.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n\342\225\224\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\227\n\342\225\221                TERMLINK                  \342\225\221\n\342\225\221          P2P COLLABORATION                \342\225\221\n\342\225\232\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\235\n\nRoom created successfully!\n\nROOM CODE\n\n                  47\n\nJoin from another terminal:\n\n    termlink 47\n\nInvite link:\n\n    https://YOUR-DOMAIN/join/47\n\nStatus: Waiting for peers...\n\nConnected members: 1\n```\n\nThe room code should be assigned dynamically.\n\nThe room should not require the user to configure a long token manually.\n\n## Joining\n\nUsers should be able to join using any of these methods:\n\n```bash\ntermlink 47\n```\n\n```bash\ntermlink join 47\n```\n\n```bash\ntermlink join https://YOUR-DOMAIN/join/47\n```\n\nAllow invitation links to open the TermLink CLI or an optional companion application if the operating system supports it.\n\n## IMPORTANT SECURITY REQUIREMENT\n\nTwo-digit codes contain only 100 possibilities.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The two-digit code is a room discovery identifier, not an authentication credential.\n- Never use the code itself as an encryption key.\n- Never use it as the sole authentication mechanism.\n- Require host approval for new members by default.\n- Rate-limit connection attempts.\n- Prevent automated room enumeration.\n- Apply short room-code lifetimes.\n- Handle code collisions safely.\n- Show the room name and host identity before the user joins.\n- Make it possible to reject unknown peers.\n- Do not expose a room to arbitrary users without permission.\n\nIf multiple rooms have the same code, handle this safely.\n\nImplement a room-resolution system. If the code is ambiguous, show available room candidates with limited metadata and require the user to select the intended room. Do not connect to a random room.\n\n## Room code lifecycle\n\n- Room code assigned when a room is created.\n- Room code becomes inactive when the room closes.\n- Rooms can optionally rotate their code.\n- Reconnection should be supported for existing authenticated members.\n- Host can disable public quick-connect.\n- Host can require a password or additional verification.\n- Host can generate stronger invitation links.\n\n---\n\n# 5. INVITATION LINKS\n\nImplement an invitation link system.\n\nLink structure should be configurable:\n\n```text\nhttps://YOUR-DOMAIN/join/47\n```\n\nAlso support a secure invitation token:\n\n```text\nhttps://YOUR-DOMAIN/invite/SECURE-TOKEN\n```\n\nThe short room code is convenient.\n\nThe secure invitation token is useful when the host wants to invite someone without exposing a room to public discovery.\n\n## Link requirements\n\n- Create join links automatically.\n- Copy link to clipboard.\n- Display link in terminal.\n- Allow opening link on another device.\n- Validate links.\n- Support expiration.\n- Support revocation.\n- Support one-time invitations.\n- Support configurable permissions.\n- Never place sensitive credentials directly in public URLs.\n- Use HTTPS for hosted invitation services.\n- Provide a configurable signaling server URL.\n- Do not hardcode a domain that the developer does not own.\n\nDuring local development, use localhost links, a documented development URL, or an invitation URI scheme.\n\nDo not pretend that a real public short-link domain exists until one is configured.\n\n---\n\n# 6. GROUP CONNECTION SYSTEM\n\nGroup connections are a fundamental feature, not an optional future addition.\n\nUsers should be able to create rooms with multiple participants.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nRoom: DEV TEAM\nCode: 47\n\nConnected peers: 5\n\n\342\227\217 AADI-PC          Host\n\342\227\217 RAHUL-LAPTOP    Member\n\342\227\217 UBUNTU-VM       Member\n\342\227\217 KALI-LINUX      Member\n\342\227\217 MACBOOK         Member\n```\n\nImplement:\n\n- Group creation\n- Group joining\n- Member discovery\n- Member list\n- Connection status\n- Host identification\n- Member identity\n- Group messaging\n- Private peer messaging\n- Group file sharing\n- Direct file transfer\n- Member removal\n- Invitation generation\n- Room closing\n- Host transfer where appropriate\n- Reconnection\n- Leave room\n- Connection diagnostics\n\n## Initial group size\n\nTarget:\n\n- 2\342\200\2238 peers: fully supported initial group size.\n- 9\342\200\22320 peers: experimental and optimized.\n- Larger groups: future scalable architecture.\n\nDo not claim arbitrary scalability without performance testing.\n\n---\n\n# 7. NETWORK ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a decentralized-first architecture.\n\nThe architecture should include:\n\n## A. CLI application\n\nRuns on every participating computer.\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- User interface\n- Local identity\n- Room management\n- Messaging\n- File operations\n- Terminal sessions\n- Permission checks\n- Peer connections\n\n## B. Signaling server\n\nA lightweight service used for:\n\n- Room discovery\n- Initial connection negotiation\n- Invitation handling\n- Peer discovery\n- Connection metadata exchange\n- Group membership coordination\n\nThe signaling server must not automatically receive the contents of private messages, files, or terminal streams.\n\n## C. P2P transport\n\nUse a suitable real peer-to-peer transport.\n\nEvaluate cross-platform Node.js options before selecting a dependency.\n\nPrefer technologies that support:\n\n- Encrypted connections\n- Reliable data channels\n- UDP connectivity\n- NAT traversal\n- Direct peer connections\n- Cross-platform Node.js support\n- Group networking\n\nPotential technologies include:\n\n- WebRTC Data Channels\n- A compatible Node.js WebRTC implementation\n- Secure peer-to-peer transport alternatives if WebRTC implementation compatibility is problematic\n\nDo not choose an unstable dependency without evaluating its compatibility.\n\n## D. STUN\n\nUse STUN to assist with NAT traversal and peer connectivity.\n\nAllow configuration of STUN servers.\n\n## E. TURN relay fallback\n\nImplement optional TURN relay fallback for situations where direct P2P cannot connect.\n\nRelay should be configurable.\n\nThe application should display whether a connection is:\n\n- Direct P2P\n- Relayed\n- Local network\n- VPN\n- Connecting\n- Disconnected\n\nDo not falsely report a direct connection when data is relayed.\n\n## F. Group topology\n\nSupport different connection strategies.\n\n### Small groups\n\nUse full mesh when practical.\n\n### Larger groups\n\nUse hybrid connectivity and selective peer connections.\n\nThe system must not blindly create unlimited peer connections.\n\nUse a configurable topology manager.\n\nFor a mesh of N peers:\n\n`connections = N \303\227 (N - 1) / 2`\n\nMonitor resource consumption.\n\nImplement a reasonable peer connection limit.\n\n---\n\n# 8. GROUP CHAT\n\nCreate a functional terminal-based chat system.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nTERMLINK CHAT\nRoom: DEV TEAM\n\n[12:30] AADI: Server started\n[12:31] RAHUL: Connection successful\n[12:31] DEV: Testing file transfer\n\nType a message:\n>\n```\n\nFeatures:\n\n- Group messaging\n- Private messages\n- Message timestamps\n- Sender names\n- System messages\n- Connection notifications\n- Member joined notifications\n- Member left notifications\n- Message delivery status where possible\n- Message history for the current session\n- Optional local history\n- Clear chat\n- Reconnect support\n\nCommands:\n\n```text\n/chat\n/msg Rahul Hello\n/members\n/status\n/clear\n/help\n/leave\n```\n\nDo not store chat history remotely by default.\n\n---\n\n# 9. P2P FILE TRANSFER\n\nImplement real file transfer.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ntermlink send ./project.zip\n```\n\nSend to the entire group:\n\n```bash\ntermlink send ./project.zip --group\n```\n\nSend to a specific peer:\n\n```bash\ntermlink send ./project.zip --to Rahul\n```\n\nTransfer prompt:\n\n```text\nIncoming file\n\nFilename: project.zip\nSize: 450 MB\nSender: AADI-PC\n\nAccept transfer? [Y/n]\n```\n\nImplement:\n\n- Single file transfer\n- Multiple file transfer\n- Group file transfer\n- Peer-specific transfer\n- File size display\n- Transfer progress\n- Transfer speed\n- Estimated remaining time\n- Pause\n- Resume where feasible\n- Cancel\n- Destination selection\n- Filename conflict handling\n- Recipient acceptance\n- Rejection\n- Chunked transfers\n- File integrity verification\n- SHA-256 verification\n- Error handling\n- Interrupted transfer recovery\n- Transfer history stored locally\n\nNever silently overwrite existing files.\n\nNever execute received files automatically.\n\n## Transfer protocol\n\nDesign a robust transfer protocol.\n\nUse chunked data transmission.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Transfer ID\n- Metadata\n- File name\n- File size\n- Chunk indexes\n- Chunk checksums\n- File checksum\n- Transfer status\n- Acknowledgements\n- Resume state\n\nAvoid loading entire large files into memory.\n\nUse streaming APIs.\n\nImplement backpressure.\n\n---\n\n# 10. PEER-ASSISTED GROUP DISTRIBUTION\n\nDesign the system so a future version can support peer-assisted file distribution.\n\nExample:\n\nA host shares a 2 GB file with 10 members.\n\nInstead of sending 10 separate full copies, recipients who receive valid chunks may optionally share those chunks with other members.\n\nImplement this as a future-ready protocol module.\n\nDo not implement unsafe or uncontrolled file redistribution.\n\nThe original sender or host must control sharing permissions.\n\n---\n\n# 11. REMOTE TERMINAL SHARING\n\nImplement terminal sharing as a major feature, but keep it disabled until explicitly enabled by the host.\n\nHost:\n\n```bash\ntermlink session share\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nTERMINAL SESSION\n\nSession: backend-server\nOwner: AADI-PC\n\nMembers:\n\n1. RAHUL-LAPTOP    Read-only\n2. DEV-PC          Read-only\n\nInteractive access: Disabled\n```\n\n## Modes\n\n### Read-only\n\nParticipants can see terminal output but cannot type commands.\n\n### Interactive\n\nApproved users can interact with the terminal.\n\n### Host-controlled\n\nThe host retains control and may grant/revoke interactive access.\n\n## Features\n\n- Terminal session sharing\n- Terminal output streaming\n- Interactive input\n- Read-only viewers\n- Host approval\n- Per-peer permission\n- Session locking\n- Session termination\n- Participant removal\n- Revoke permissions immediately\n- Connection-loss handling\n- Session audit records\n- Multiple sessions where practical\n\n## Cross-platform shell support\n\nWindows:\n\n- PowerShell\n- Windows Terminal environments\n- cmd.exe as optional compatibility\n\nLinux:\n\n- Bash\n- Zsh\n- Other configured shells\n\nmacOS:\n\n- Zsh\n- Bash\n- Configured shell\n\nUse a PTY abstraction.\n\nUse platform-specific adapters only when necessary.\n\nDo not assume Bash exists on Windows.\n\nDo not assume PowerShell exists on Linux.\n\n## SECURITY REQUIREMENTS\n\nRemote shell access must never be enabled automatically.\n\nBefore granting access:\n\n1. Host explicitly starts the session.\n2. Host chooses permitted participants.\n3. Host chooses read-only or interactive mode.\n4. Participants receive an access notification.\n5. Host can revoke access instantly.\n\nNo hidden shell execution.\n\nNo silent remote commands.\n\nNo background remote access without explicit user consent.\n\nDo not execute commands received from another peer unless the user has explicitly granted interactive access.\n\n---\n\n# 12. COMMAND SHARING\n\nImplement a safe command-sharing feature.\n\nUsers can send commands to another participant for review.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCommand proposal from AADI-PC:\n\nnpm install\n\nExecute?\n\n[Accept] [Reject] [Edit]\n```\n\nBy default:\n\n- Commands are suggestions only.\n- Commands are not automatically executed.\n- The recipient reviews commands.\n- The recipient explicitly approves execution.\n- Host can disable command sharing.\n- Dangerous commands should receive additional warnings.\n- Maintain local audit records.\n\nNever execute a received command automatically.\n\n---\n\n# 13. USER IDENTITY\n\nImplement lightweight local identities.\n\nEach installation should be able to generate a cryptographic identity.\n\nIdentity includes:\n\n- Public key\n- Private key stored locally\n- Device name\n- Platform\n- Unique device identifier\n- Peer fingerprint\n\nThe private key must never be transmitted.\n\nDisplay identity verification when connecting to a peer.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nPeer:\n\nName: RAHUL-LAPTOP\nPlatform: Windows\nFingerprint: A2:9F:81:7B...\n\nTrust this peer?\n\n[Y] Trust\n[N] Reject\n```\n\nAllow:\n\n- Trusted devices\n- Revoked devices\n- Identity verification\n- Device renaming\n- Local identity reset\n- Identity export where safe\n- Trust management\n\nDo not rely solely on a username or room code for authentication.\n\n---\n\n# 14. SECURITY AND PRIVACY\n\nSecurity is a first-class requirement.\n\nImplement:\n\n- Encrypted transport\n- Peer authentication\n- Cryptographic identity\n- Secure random token generation\n- Invitation expiration\n- Host approval\n- Connection rate limiting\n- Secure room management\n- File-transfer acceptance\n- Remote shell permissions\n- Local secret storage\n- Input validation\n- Safe filenames\n- Path traversal prevention\n- Symlink handling\n- Resource limits\n- Connection timeout\n- Abuse protection\n- Logging controls\n\n## Threat model\n\nConsider:\n\n- Unauthorized room joining\n- Code guessing\n- Room enumeration\n- Impersonation\n- Malicious peers\n- Malicious files\n- Path traversal\n- File overwrite\n- Denial of service\n- Oversized payloads\n- Connection flooding\n- Malicious command execution\n- Signaling server compromise\n- Relay server compromise\n- Peer identity spoofing\n\nDo not claim that the system is completely secure.\n\nDocument limitations and security assumptions.\n\n---\n\n# 15. PERMISSION SYSTEM\n\nCreate role-based permissions.\n\n## Owner\n\n- Create room\n- Close room\n- Invite peers\n- Remove peers\n- Grant permissions\n- Transfer ownership\n- Share files\n- Create terminal sessions\n- Grant terminal access\n- Manage administrators\n\n## Admin\n\n- Manage participants\n- Invite peers\n- Moderate chat\n- Approve transfers\n- Manage permissions within allowed limits\n\n## Collaborator\n\n- Chat\n- Transfer files\n- Participate in approved terminal sessions\n\n## Viewer\n\n- Chat if enabled\n- View approved terminal sessions\n- Receive files\n\n## Guest\n\n- Limited access\n- Host approval\n- Restricted file access\n- No terminal execution privileges\n\nPermissions must be configurable.\n\n---\n\n# 16. CLI DESIGN\n\nCreate a professional CLI.\n\nUse a clean, intuitive command structure.\n\n## Main command\n\n```bash\ntermlink\n```\n\nLaunch an interactive menu.\n\nMenu:\n\n```text\n\342\225\224\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\227\n\342\225\221             TERMLINK              \342\225\221\n\342\225\232\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\220\342\225\235\n\n1. Create a room\n2. Join a room\n3. Chat\n4. Transfer files\n5. Share terminal\n6. Trusted devices\n7. Network diagnostics\n8. Settings\n9. Exit\n```\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\ntermlink\ntermlink 47\ntermlink create\ntermlink host\ntermlink join 47\ntermlink join LINK\ntermlink room create\ntermlink room close\ntermlink room members\ntermlink room invite\ntermlink room settings\ntermlink chat\ntermlink send FILE\ntermlink send FILE --group\ntermlink send FILE --to PEER\ntermlink receive\ntermlink session share\ntermlink session list\ntermlink session stop\ntermlink peers\ntermlink devices\ntermlink trust\ntermlink diagnostics\ntermlink config\ntermlink status\ntermlink update\ntermlink version\ntermlink help\n```\n\nAdd proper argument validation.\n\nUse helpful errors.\n\nProvide autocomplete where feasible.\n\nProvide a non-interactive mode for scripts.\n\n---\n\n# 17. TERMINAL UI\n\nCreate a modern terminal user interface.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Clear visual hierarchy\n- Cross-platform rendering\n- Colors where supported\n- Graceful fallback for basic terminals\n- Keyboard navigation\n- Progress bars\n- Connection indicators\n- Member list\n- Notifications\n- Help\n- Responsive layouts\n- Unicode support with fallback\n- No excessive animations\n- Accessibility\n\nUse a stable CLI framework and terminal UI framework.\n\nPossible tools:\n\n- Commander.js\n- Ink\n- Ora\n- Chalk\n- Prompts\n- Cli-progress\n\nChoose compatible libraries and avoid unnecessary dependencies.\n\n---\n\n# 18. NETWORK DIAGNOSTICS\n\nAdd:\n\n```bash\ntermlink diagnostics\n```\n\nIt should report:\n\n- Node.js version\n- TermLink version\n- Operating system\n- Network interfaces\n- Local connectivity\n- Signaling server connectivity\n- STUN test\n- Direct connection test\n- Relay availability\n- Firewall warning\n- NAT information where available\n- Port information where applicable\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nTERMLINK DIAGNOSTICS\n\nPlatform: Windows 11\nNode: 22.x\nConnection: Internet\n\nSignaling: Connected\nSTUN: Successful\nDirect P2P: Available\nRelay: Available\n\nStatus: Ready\n```\n\nDo not display sensitive IP addresses unless the user requests detailed diagnostics.\n\n---\n\n# 19. ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a modular monorepo.\n\nSuggested structure:\n\n```text\ntermlink/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 apps/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 cli/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 signaling-server/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 packages/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 core/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 identity/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 protocol/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 networking/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 room-engine/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 file-transfer/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 terminal-session/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 permissions/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 diagnostics/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 sdk/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 tests/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 integration/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 networking/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 cross-platform/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 security/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docs/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 installation.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 usage.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 architecture.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 networking.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 security.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 troubleshooting.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 development.md\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 contributing.md\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 examples/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 scripts/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 .github/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 workflows/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 README.md\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LICENSE\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 CONTRIBUTING.md\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 SECURITY.md\n```\n\nUse a workspace-based monorepo.\n\nUse a clear dependency structure.\n\nAvoid circular dependencies.\n\nSeparate networking, user interface, protocol logic, and platform-specific terminal functionality.\n\n---\n\n# 20. TECHNOLOGY STACK\n\nUse TypeScript.\n\nRecommended:\n\n- Node.js LTS\n- TypeScript\n- npm workspaces\n- Commander.js\n- Ink or another suitable CLI UI framework\n- WebSocket signaling\n- WebRTC-compatible P2P transport\n- STUN\n- Optional TURN\n- Node.js streaming APIs\n- PTY abstraction\n- Vitest or Node.js test runner\n- ESLint\n- Prettier\n- GitHub Actions\n- tsup or another suitable build system\n\nResearch compatibility before selecting the networking and PTY dependencies.\n\nEnsure that dependencies work on Windows, Linux and macOS.\n\nAvoid packages that only support one operating system unless they are isolated behind a platform adapter.\n\n---\n\n# 21. SDK\n\nExpose a developer SDK.\n\nExample:\n\n```typescript\nimport { TermLink } from \"termlink\";\n\nconst client = new TermLink();\n\nawait client.initialize();\n\nconst room = await client.createRoom();\n\nconsole.log(room.code);\n\nroom.on(\"peerJoined\", (peer) => {\n  console.log(`${peer.name} connected`);\n});\n\nroom.on(\"message\", (message) => {\n  console.log(message.text);\n});\n\nawait room.sendMessage(\"Hello, everyone!\");\n```\n\nCreate a well-defined public API.\n\nSupport:\n\n- Room creation\n- Room joining\n- Disconnect\n- Messages\n- Peer events\n- File transfer\n- Permissions\n- Connection status\n- Peer identity\n- Room information\n\nProvide TypeScript declarations.\n\nDocument the API.\n\nDo not expose internal implementation details unnecessarily.\n\n---\n\n# 22. CONFIGURATION\n\nSupport configuration.\n\nExample:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"deviceName\": \"AADI-PC\",\n  \"signalingServer\": \"wss://YOUR-SERVER\",\n  \"stunServers\": [],\n  \"turnServers\": [],\n  \"defaultRoomMode\": \"quick\",\n  \"requireHostApproval\": true,\n  \"maxPeers\": 8,\n  \"fileTransferDirectory\": \"./downloads\",\n  \"autoAcceptFiles\": false,\n  \"terminalSharingEnabled\": false,\n  \"telemetryEnabled\": false\n}\n```\n\nConfiguration must be validated.\n\nProvide:\n\n```bash\ntermlink config\ntermlink config set deviceName AADI-PC\ntermlink config reset\n```\n\nNever store private keys in plain configuration files.\n\nUse platform-appropriate secure storage where possible.\n\n---\n\n# 23. INSTALLATION AND DISTRIBUTION\n\nThe package must be installable through npm.\n\nGlobal installation:\n\n```bash\nnpm install -g termlink\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```bash\ntermlink\n```\n\nAlso support:\n\n```bash\nnpx termlink\n```\n\nCreate:\n\n- npm package metadata\n- Executable bin entry\n- Versioning\n- Build scripts\n- Release scripts\n- Package validation\n- npm publishing instructions\n- README installation instructions\n- Platform-specific troubleshooting\n\nDo not publish automatically without the developer\'s approval.\n\nUse a test package or dry-run during development.\n\nValidate with:\n\n```bash\nnpm pack --dry-run\n```\n\n---\n\n# 24. TESTING\n\nCreate a complete test suite.\n\n## Unit tests\n\n- Room codes\n- Invitation tokens\n- Identity\n- Permissions\n- Protocol messages\n- File chunking\n- File validation\n- Configuration\n- Error handling\n\n## Integration tests\n\n- Create room\n- Join room\n- Multiple peers\n- Messaging\n- Disconnect\n- Reconnect\n- Transfer files\n- Transfer cancellation\n- Host removal\n- Permission revocation\n\n## Cross-platform tests\n\nUse GitHub Actions.\n\nTest at least:\n\n- Windows\n- Ubuntu Linux\n- macOS\n\nUse supported Node.js versions.\n\n## Networking tests\n\nTest:\n\n- Same LAN\n- Different networks\n- NAT\n- Relay fallback\n- Network loss\n- Reconnection\n- Peer disappearance\n- Signaling failure\n- Duplicate room codes\n\n## Security tests\n\nTest:\n\n- Invalid invitations\n- Expired tokens\n- Code guessing rate limits\n- Unauthorized shell requests\n- Malicious file names\n- Path traversal\n- Oversized files\n- Unauthorized peer actions\n\nDo not claim a test passed unless it was actually executed.\n\n---\n\n# 25. GITHUB AND CI/CD\n\nCreate GitHub Actions workflows.\n\n## CI\n\nOn:\n\n- Pull requests\n- Pushes\n- Release branches\n\nRun:\n\n1. Install dependencies\n2. Type check\n3. Lint\n4. Unit tests\n5. Integration tests\n6. Build\n7. Package validation\n\nMatrix:\n\n- Windows\n- Ubuntu\n- macOS\n\n## Release\n\nCreate a controlled release workflow.\n\n- Version validation\n- Changelog\n- Git tags\n- Build\n- Test\n- npm publish approval\n- GitHub release\n\nDo not expose npm publishing credentials.\n\nUse secure GitHub secrets or trusted publishing.\n\n---\n\n# 26. DOCUMENTATION\n\nWrite a complete README.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Project description\n- Features\n- Screenshots or terminal examples\n- Installation\n- Quick start\n- Create room\n- Join room\n- Chat\n- File transfer\n- Group sharing\n- Terminal sharing\n- Permissions\n- Windows setup\n- Linux setup\n- macOS setup\n- VMware setup\n- VirtualBox setup\n- Troubleshooting\n- Network requirements\n- Security\n- Roadmap\n- Contribution guide\n- License\n\nCreate detailed docs for:\n\n- Architecture\n- Networking\n- Protocol\n- Security\n- Developer SDK\n- Installation\n- Troubleshooting\n- Self-hosting signaling\n- TURN configuration\n- Terminal-sharing permissions\n\n---\n\n# 27. VMWARE AND VIRTUAL MACHINE DOCUMENTATION\n\nDocument the following scenarios.\n\n## Scenario A\n\nWindows host + Ubuntu VMware guest.\n\nBoth run TermLink.\n\n## Scenario B\n\nWindows host + Kali VMware guest + Ubuntu VM.\n\nAll join the same group.\n\n## Scenario C\n\nDifferent physical computers and VMs across separate networks.\n\nUse direct connectivity where possible and relay fallback when necessary.\n\n## Scenario D\n\nHost-only networking.\n\nDocument that host-only networks may not have internet access and may only permit connections between machines on the virtual network.\n\n## Scenario E\n\nNAT networking.\n\nExplain NAT traversal limitations and how relay fallback may help.\n\nProvide clear troubleshooting commands.\n\n---\n\n# 28. USER EXPERIENCE REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe product should feel polished.\n\nUse:\n\n- Clear onboarding\n- Easy joining\n- Minimal typing\n- Two-digit codes\n- Invitation links\n- Easy clipboard copying\n- Host approval\n- Clear connection state\n- File transfer progress\n- Human-readable errors\n- Helpful troubleshooting\n- Consistent terminal UI\n\nAvoid:\n\n- Complicated configuration\n- Unnecessary login\n- Long default codes\n- Confusing technical errors\n- Fake connection states\n- Fake file transfers\n- Fake terminal sharing\n- Unnecessary cloud uploads\n\n---\n\n# 29. FUTURE FEATURES\n\nPrepare architecture for:\n\n- Voice chat\n- Video chat\n- Screen sharing\n- GUI desktop application\n- Mobile companion\n- Android\n- Remote development\n- Shared project folders\n- Collaborative coding\n- Plugin system\n- Custom transport providers\n- Enterprise groups\n- Persistent rooms\n- Team management\n- Peer-assisted file distribution\n- Anonymous temporary rooms\n- Custom room domains\n- Device trust management\n- Web interface\n- Terminal session recording\n- Local AI integration\n- AI-assisted command explanations\n\nThese must not interfere with the core product.\n\n---\n\n# 30. DEVELOPMENT APPROACH\n\nDo not attempt to create every feature in a single unverified step.\n\nDevelop incrementally.\n\n## PHASE 1 \342\200\224 FOUNDATION\n\nBuild:\n\n- Repository\n- Workspace\n- TypeScript\n- CLI\n- Configuration\n- Logging\n- Cross-platform compatibility\n- Build process\n- Tests\n\nDeliver a working CLI.\n\n## PHASE 2 \342\200\224 SIGNALING AND ROOMS\n\nBuild:\n\n- Signaling server\n- Room creation\n- Two-digit room code system\n- Invitation links\n- Join flow\n- Host approval\n- Member list\n- Room lifecycle\n- Collision handling\n\nDeliver real room functionality.\n\n## PHASE 3 \342\200\224 P2P CONNECTION\n\nBuild:\n\n- Peer discovery\n- Connection negotiation\n- P2P transport\n- Encryption\n- Peer identity\n- Connection status\n- Direct connection\n- Relay fallback\n\nVerify actual peer connectivity.\n\n## PHASE 4 \342\200\224 GROUP CHAT\n\nBuild:\n\n- Messaging\n- Group events\n- Private messages\n- Chat UI\n- Reconnection\n\nVerify messaging across operating systems.\n\n## PHASE 5 \342\200\224 FILE TRANSFER\n\nBuild:\n\n- File transfer protocol\n- Streaming\n- Chunking\n- Progress\n- Recipient approval\n- Integrity\n- Cancellation\n- Recovery\n\nVerify actual files are transferred.\n\n## PHASE 6 \342\200\224 PERMISSIONS\n\nBuild:\n\n- Roles\n- Host approval\n- Admin\n- Guest\n- Permission controls\n- Peer removal\n- Trust management\n"
    num_lines: 1818
    num_bytes: 33951
    line_range_bytes: 28891
  }
}
