  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Needs**\n\nI\'ve got the development environment setup with Node v22.23.2 and npm 10.9.8 on Linux x86_64. I\'m carefully analyzing the user\'s request and figuring out how to construct a genuine and working, production-ready TermLink platform that satisfies all requirements. I\'m focusing on user satisfaction and a high-quality product.\n\n\n**Defining Project Scope**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a much clearer picture now. I\'m building a complete TermLink monorepo \342\200\223 it\'s going to be a real, runnable CLI, not just a concept. We\'re talking end-to-end encryption with Ed25519, X25519, and AES-256-GCM. I\'m focusing on WebSocket signaling, P2P transport, chunked file transfers, terminal session sharing, robust security, and full documentation.\n\n\n**Constructing Real Architecture**\n\nI\'m now deep in architecting the TermLink Web Companion and Multi-Peer Control Console. I\'m focusing on port 3000, ensuring it\'s a live, full-stack setup with Express, a WebSocket Signaling Server, and Vite middleware. The React UI will be served at `http://localhost:3000`, `/join/:code`, and `/invite/:token`. The real WebSocket signaling will be at `ws://localhost:3000/ws`. I\'m especially focused on the power of this dual architecture, with the browser companion connecting directly to the same signaling server.\n\n\n**Testing Interactive Workflow**\n\nI\'m now implementing the interactive workspace on port 3000 in the AI Studio preview. I\'m focusing on ensuring users can create and join rooms, generating real 2-digit codes, join links, and invite tokens. I will ensure all connections use real peers via WebSockets/WebRTC, never faking or simulating their operation. The focus is on a fully functional CLI and supporting real P2P file transfers and group chat functionality directly.\n\n\n**Implementing Advanced Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating robust binary transfers, focusing on chunked streaming with a recipient accept/reject modal, a progress bar displaying speed and ETA, and SHA-256 verification. I\'m also adding pause/resume/cancel functionality and conflict-safe download handling. I\'m building remote terminal sharing capabilities, which are disabled by default. The host will start a real server-backed shell session, which they can choose to be read-only or interactive, and revoke instantly, with an audit log viewable. I am developing a command proposal system, which flags potentially dangerous commands.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Package Choices**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on package selections. I\'m focusing on rock-solid, cross-platform stability. My priority is a WebSocket library like `ws` and a CLI framework such as `commander`. I am also including `chalk` for terminal colors with graceful fallback. I\'m carefully avoiding native C++ addons like `node-pty` or `wrtc` because they often cause compilation problems on multiple platforms, especially with containers. My networking plan involves native `RTCPeerConnection` and `RTCDataChannel` in browsers, with fallback to an E2EE envelope over WebSocket if direct ICE connections fail.\n\n\n**Establishing Direct Connections**\n\nI\'m now focused on the Node.js CLI/SDK\'s transport layer. My approach involves direct encrypted TCP/UDP communication, utilizing Noise-like X25519 ECDH, AES-256-GCM, and Ed25519 for authenticated P2P transport. I am working towards LAN/Direct TCP hole-punching and STUN UDP endpoint discovery using native Node.js `dgram`. A fallback E2EE WebSocket relay, where the server is blind to session keys, is also being implemented.\n\n\n**Refining Transport Security**\n\nI\'m thrilled with the transport layer progress, achieving direct encrypted TCP/UDP with fallback to a blind E2EE WebSocket relay. This handles all network scenarios transparently, accurately reporting connection types, like \"Direct P2P\" or \"Relayed.\" The focus is on robust PTY abstraction for interactive shell sessions, using `PtyAdapter` in the `terminal-session` package. I\'m dynamically detecting platform shells, including `powershell.exe`, and Unix shells, and using either a `python3 -c pty.spawn` or `script` based PTY wrapper when available, falling back to a pipe of `child_process.spawn`. I have included dynamic `node-pty` plugin detection if installed by the user.\n\n\n**Formulating Verification Plan**\n\nMy focus is now on the verification plan for `ultra-mode`. I\'m detailing thorough unit and integration tests. I will use `tsx --test`. Unit tests will cover room codes, cryptography, permissions, invite tokens, config validation, and dangerous command detection. Integration tests will simulate multi-peer sessions.\n\n\n**Defining Test Strategies**\n\nI\'m now detailing test strategies for the interactive workspace, focusing on thorough verification of end-to-end functionality. I am creating specific tests to ensure peers can join via 2-digit codes, the file transfer system works, disconnect events function, reconnects are seamless, and that unauthorized peers are rejected. I will focus on security aspects such as rate limiting and blocked exploits.\n\n\n\n**Validating Real Use Cases**\n\nI\'m now deep in use-case validation. I\'m focusing on real CLI process invocation and `npm pack` validation. I am also planning a series of browser journeys via `playwright-cli` on `http://localhost:3000`. I\'m starting with a desktop first-paint and create room journey to ensure the workspace renders cleanly. After that, I will conduct a multi-peer real-time collaboration journey with two live clients using real WebSocket connections.\n\n\n**Verifying Interactive Workflows**\n\nI\'m now focusing on verifying all the interactive workspaces. I need to switch to Files, Terminal Sharing, Devices, Diagnostics, and Settings views. I\'m checking that every view renders real data and functioning controls without errors. I\'m also ensuring the Mobile Responsive Reflow functions, and that resizing to 390x844 properly collapses navigation without overflow. I am testing the Room Lobby, Group Chat, P2P File Transfer, Remote Terminal Sharing, and Trusted Devices features.\n\n\n\n**Designing Visual Elements**\n\nI\'m now deep in defining the visual design for the TermLink Web Companion. My focus is on a crisp, dark-slate aesthetic with high contrast for accessibility. I\'m prioritizing the single emerald-teal accent color for primary actions, ensuring no distracting elements like decorative pulsing dots. I\'m also going with clear typography choices for optimal readability. I want this to be a functional, user-friendly interface.\n\n\n"
